Given, length, $l=11 \mathrm{~m}$ Radius, $\quad r=2 \mathrm{~cm}$ Radius, $\quad=2 \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{~m}$
Mass, $m=40$ tons

=4 \times 10^{4} \mathrm{~kg}

Increase in length, $\Delta l=$ ?
We know that, Young's modulus,

\begin{aligned}
Y &=\frac{m g l}{\pi r^{2}(\Delta l)} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \Delta l &=\frac{m g l}{\pi r^{2} Y} \\
&=\frac{4 \times 10^{4} \times 10 \times 11}{3.14 \times\left(2 \times 10^{-2}\right)^{2} \times 2 \times 10^{11}} \\
&=1.75 \times 10^{-2} \mathrm{~m} \\
&=1.75 \mathrm{~cm}
\end{aligned}

We know that, Young's modulus of elasticity,

\begin{array}{ll} 
& Y=\frac{F L}{\pi r^{2} l} \quad \text { [where, } l \text { is change in length] } \\
\Rightarrow \quad & l=\frac{F L}{\pi r^{2} Y} \\
\Rightarrow \quad & l \propto \frac{L}{r^{2}} \propto \frac{L}{d^{2}} \quad \text { [where, } d=\text { diameter] } \\
\Rightarrow \quad & l \propto \frac{L}{d^{2}}
\end{array}

Among the given options, $\frac{L}{d^{2}}$ is maximum for the values of $L=50 \mathrm{~cm}$ and $d=0.5 \mathrm{~mm}$.

Given, change in length of wire, $l_{1}=2 \mathrm{~mm}, l_{2}=$ ?, length of wire, $L_{1}=2 \mathrm{~m}$ and length of another wire, $L_{2}=8 \mathrm{~m}$
Change in length, $l=\frac{F L}{A Y}=\frac{F L^{2}}{(A \cdot L) Y}=\frac{F L^{2}}{V Y}[V=A \cdot L]$
where, $Y$ is Young's modulus.

\begin{aligned}
&\therefore \quad l \propto L^{2} \quad(\text { as } V, Y \text { and } F \text { are constants) } \\
&\frac{l_{2}}{l_{1}}=\left(\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{8}{2}\right)^{2}=16 \\
&\Rightarrow \quad l_{2}=16 l_{1}=16 \times 2 \mathrm{~mm} \\
&=32 \mathrm{~mm}=3.2 \mathrm{~cm}
\end{aligned}

Young's modulus, $Y=\frac{F L}{A \Delta L}=\frac{4 F L}{\pi D^{2} \Delta L}\Rightarrow \quad \Delta L=\frac{4 F L}{\pi D^{2} Y}$
The ratio of increase in length of steel and copper is $\frac{\Delta L_{s}}{\Delta L_{c}}=\frac{F_{s} L_{s}}{D_{s}^{2} Y_{s}} \times \frac{D_{c}^{2} Y_{c}}{F_{c} L_{c}}=\frac{F_{s}}{F_{c}} \times \frac{L_{s}}{L_{c}} \times\left(\frac{D_{c}}{D_{s}}\right)^{2} \times \frac{Y_{c}}{Y_{s}}$
Force on steel wire, $F_{s}=(2 m+5 m) g=7 m g$
Force on copper wire, $F_{c}=5 \mathrm{mg}$
\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \frac{F_{s}}{F_{c}}=\frac{7}{5} \\
\text { Also, } & \frac{D_{s}}{D_{c}}=p, \frac{L_{s}}{L_{c}}=q, \frac{Y_{s}}{Y_{c}}=s \\
\therefore \quad & \frac{\Delta L_{s}}{\Delta L_{c}}=\frac{7}{5} \times q \times \frac{1}{p^{2}} \times \frac{1}{s}=\frac{7 q}{5 s p^{2}}
\end{array}
